The solutions are any functions with  $f(0) \leq 0 \leq f(1)$  and  $f(x) = 0$  for all  $x \in (0,1)$ . 

They satisfy the inequality for all  $n$  since the integral is always  $0$ . To show that there are no other solutions we can argue by contradiction:

If for any  $a \in [0, 1)$  we had  $f(a) > 0$  then since  $f$  is nondecreasing we would get
\begin{align*}
\int_0^1 f(x) e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x &=  \int_0^a f(x) e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x + \int_a^1 f(x) e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x
&\geq f(0)\int_0^a  e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x + f(a)\int_a^1  e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x
&=\frac{f(a)e^n +(f(0)-f(a))e^{an} -f(0)}{n}
\end{align*}
and that last term diverges to  $\infty$ , violating the upper bound of  $2008$ . Thus only  $f(1)$  can be positive. Similarly if for any  $a \in (0, 1]$  we had  $f(a) < 0$  then we would get
\begin{align*}
\int_0^1 f(x) e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x &=  \int_0^a f(x) e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x + \int_a^1 f(x) e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x
&\leq f(a)\int_0^a  e^{nx}\mathrm{d} x + 0
&=\frac{f(a)(e^{an} -1)}{n}
\end{align*}
where we have used the fact that  $f$  is nonpositive except possibly at a single point. The last term diverges to  $-\infty$ , again violating the upper bound. Thus only  $f(0)$  can be negative.

