We claim that the solution is 
\[f(x)=\begin{cases}
 x-1 \ \ \ \ \ \textrm{when} \ x \  \textrm{is odd, }
 x+1 \ \ \ \ \ \textrm{when} \ x \  \textrm{is even.}
\end{cases}\] 
We now prove that it is the only solution, notice that we have   $f(0)\geq 1, f(1) \geq 0, f(2) \geq 3, f(3) \geq 2, f(4) \geq 5, f(5) \geq 4, f(6) \geq 7, \cdots$  and since  $f$  is an surjective function,  $f(1)=0$ , otherwise there are no such  $x$  that gets mapped to  $0$ . Similarly,  $f(0)$  must be mapped to  $1$  and  $f(3)$  must be mapped to  $2$ . Inductively, we can assume that for all  $x \leq n$ , our function applies, then for  $x=n+1$ , we have two cases, when  $x$  is odd and even. **<span style="color:#f00">Case 1:</span>**  $x$  is odd. So,  $f(n+1) \geq n,f(n+2) \geq n+3$  and  $f(n+3) \geq n+2$ . Also, we have  $f(n)=n+1, f(n-1)=n-2$  and  $f(n-2)=n-1$ . Thus, we can see that  $f(n+1)$  must be  $n$ .  **<span style="color:#f00">Case 2:</span>**  $x$  is even. This is analogous to the first case.  
At last, the function  $f(x)$  clearly satisfies the inequality.  $\blacksquare$

