*Proof.* Collinearity of  $P,E$  and  $F$  comes from the angle chase;
\begin{align*}
\measuredangle EPL=\measuredangle  EBL=\measuredangle  KLC=\measuredangle FCL=\measuredangle FPL,
\end{align*}where we used the fact that  $AL\parallel BE\parallel CF$ .
Collinearity of  $K,E$  and  $F$  comes from the similarity;
\begin{align*} \frac{KL}{EB}=\frac{LC}{BL}=\frac{FC}{KL},\end{align*}
which implies the collinearity along with  $KL\parallel BE\parallel CF$  (this is a well-known lemma).**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $\triangle ARK\sim\triangle ALB$ .
*Proof.* Firstly,  $K$  lies on  $(AQRP)$  as  $\measuredangle KPR=\measuredangle FPR=\measuredangle CFR=\measuredangle KAR$ .
Now, since  $\measuredangle ARL=\measuredangle CRL=\measuredangle CPL=\measuredangle LKB=\measuredangle  AKB$  and  $\measuredangle BAK=LAR$ , we conclude that  $\triangle ABK\sim\triangle ALR$ , hence  $A$  is the center of spiral similarity sending  $\overline{BK}$  to  $\overline{LR}$ , ergo  $A$  is also the center of spiral similarity sending  $\overline{BL}$  to  $\overline{KR}$ , the claim follows.

Therefore,  $\measuredangle APK=\measuredangle ARK=\measuredangle ALB=\measuredangle FCL=\measuredangle FPL=\measuredangle KPL\implies \overline{KP}$  bisects  $\angle APL$ . Hence,   $$ \frac{AP}{PL}=\frac{AK}{KL}=\frac{AK}{KN}. $$  On top of that  $\measuredangle AKN=\measuredangle LKN=2\measuredangle ALB=2\measuredangle APK=\measuredangle APL$ , hence we conclude that  $\triangle AKN\sim\triangle APL$ . By gliding principle as  $M,D$  are the midpoints of  $\overline{KP},\overline{NL}$ , respectively,  $\triangle AKN\sim\triangle APL\sim\triangle AMD$ . Finally, \begin{align*}
\measuredangle AMD=\measuredangle APL=2\measuredangle ALB=\measuredangle ACB+\measuredangle ABC.
\end{align*} We are done.

Let  $v_{p}\bigl(LCM(1,2,\ldots,n+1)\bigr)=v_{p}(RHS)=k$ . It is enough if we show  $v_{p}(LHS)=k$ . Since  $v_{p}(RHS)=k$  therefore we have  $p^{k}\leq n+1 <p^{k+1}$ . Note that we have \[(n+1)LCM\!\left\{\binom{n}{1},\binom{n}{2},\ldots,\binom{n}{n}\right\}=LCM_{1\leq i\leq n}\left\{(n+1)\binom{n}{i}\right\}\tag{1}\] If  $n+1=p^k$  then note that \[v_{p}\left((n+1)\binom{n}{i}\right)=v_{p}(n+1)+v_{p}\binom{n}{i} =k+v_{p}\binom{n}{i}\geq k \implies v_{p}(LHS)\geq k\tag{2}\] So our goal now is to show  $v_{p}(LHS)\leq k$ . It is enough to show that  $v_{p}$  of every term in the  $LHS$  is  $\leq k$ . Consider an arbitrary term in the  $LHS$ . It is of the form \[(n+1)\binom{n}{i}=(i+1)\binom{n+1}{i+1}\] Let  $v_{p}(i+1)=a$ . Then we have  $i+1=p^{a}b$  where  $p\nmid b$ . We have
\begin{align*}
v_{p}\left((i+1)\binom{n+1}{i+1}\right) &=v_{p}(i+1) + v_{p}\binom{n+1}{i+1}
 &= a + \sum_{t=1}^{k}\left(\left[\frac{n+1}{p^t}\right]-\left[\frac{i+1}{p^t}\right]-\left[\frac{n-i}{p^t}\right]\right)\tag{3}
\end{align*}
Note that for any  $x,y\in\mathbb{R}$  we have  $[x+y]-[x]-[y]\in \{0,1\}$ . Using the fact that  $n+1=(i+1)+(n-i)$  we deduce that the quantity   $$ f_{p}(t)=\left[\frac{n+1}{p^t}\right]-\left[\frac{i+1}{p^t}\right]-\left[\frac{n-i}{p^t}\right] \in\{0,1\} $$   We now claim that  $f_{p}(t)=0$  whenever  $t\in\{1,2,\ldots,a\}$ . Note that whenever  $t\in\{1,2,\ldots,a\}$   $p^t$  divides  $i+1$ . Write  $i+1=xp^{t}$ . Write  $n-i=yp^{t}+r$  where  $0\leq r<p^t$ . This gives us  $n+1=(x+y)p^{t}+r$ . And so we get   $$ \left[\frac{n+1}{p^t}\right]=x+y,\left[\frac{i+1}{p^t}\right]=x,\left[\frac{n-i}{p^t}\right]=y \implies f_{p}(t)=0 $$   We deduce that as  $t$  varies from  $1$  to  $k$  the quantity  $f_{p}(t)$  is  $0$  **atleast  $a$  times** and since  $f_{p}(t)$  either takes the value  $0$  or  $1$  we deduce that  $f_{p}(t)$  is 1 **atmost**  $k-a$  times. Thus we get   $$ \sum_{t=1}^{k}\left(\left[\frac{n+1}{p^t}\right]-\left[\frac{i+1}{p^t}\right]-\left[\frac{n-i}{p^t}\right]\right)\leq k-a $$   and using (3) we get   $$ v_{p}\left((i+1)\binom{n+1}{i+1}\right)\leq a + k-a \leq k\implies v_{p}(LHS)\leq k $$

